Contoso has already onboarded 120 on-premises Windows and Linux servers to Azure Arc, and Microsoft Defender for Servers Plan 2 is enabled. The operations team must now apply approved operating system updates to those servers on the second Saturday of each month between 01:00 and 05:00, and must be able to report on which machines are missing updates between those windows.

The solution must use native Azure capabilities and must cover the Arc-enabled servers and the Azure virtual machines in the same way.

What should you configure?
回答は正解です
Azure Update Manager with periodic assessment enabled on the machines and a scheduled patching maintenance configuration for the monthly window.
説明
This answer is correct. Periodic assessment makes each machine report its update compliance on a recurring basis, which provides the reporting the team needs between windows. A maintenance configuration with a schedule defines the recurring Saturday window and applies the approved updates to both Arc-enabled servers and Azure virtual machines.
An Azure Automation runbook that invokes the operating system update command on each machine on a schedule.
説明
This answer is incorrect. A custom runbook recreates functionality Azure Update Manager already provides, including compliance reporting and reboot handling. It also adds ongoing script maintenance for two different operating systems.
A Microsoft Defender for Cloud recommendation-based remediation task for missing system updates.
説明
This answer is incorrect. Defender for Cloud surfaces recommendations about missing updates but does not schedule and apply patches within a defined maintenance window. It reports the gap rather than closing it.
Azure Policy with a DeployIfNotExists effect that installs the Azure Monitor Agent on every machine.
説明
This answer is incorrect. Installing an agent is at most a prerequisite step and performs no patching. Azure Update Manager also does not depend on the Azure Monitor Agent to assess or install updates.
全体的な説明
Azure Update Manager is the native service for assessing and installing operating system updates, and it treats Arc-enabled servers and Azure virtual machines through the same interface. Periodic assessment is the setting that makes each machine re-evaluate its update status on a regular cadence so that compliance reporting stays current between patch runs. Scheduled patching is expressed as a maintenance configuration, which carries the recurrence, the window length, and the classifications of updates to install. Defender for Cloud can surface the resulting compliance gap as a recommendation, but it does not perform scheduled installation itself. Custom runbooks and agent-deployment policies either duplicate the service or address an unrelated prerequisite.
