You are the security administrator for a Microsoft Entra ID tenant. Users currently can consent to any third-party application that requests permissions. Following a review, you must implement a new policy that meets these requirements:

- Users can grant consent themselves only if the application was published by a verified publisher AND requests only permissions that your security team has classified as low risk.
- Any consent request that does not meet both conditions must be forwarded to an administrator for approval.
- Existing functionality for approved low-risk scenarios must continue to work without administrator intervention.

What should you configure in Microsoft Entra ID to meet these requirements?
Create a Conditional Access policy that blocks access to the Microsoft Entra admin center for all users except administrators, and rely on default consent settings.
説明
This answer is incorrect. Blocking access to the admin center does not control OAuth consent behavior for applications and has no effect on how users consent to app permissions. This option does not address the stated requirements at all.
Set the user consent setting to "Do not allow user consent," and enable the admin consent workflow so that all requests are routed to reviewers.
説明
This answer is incorrect. Setting user consent to "Do not allow user consent" removes the ability for users to consent to any application, which contradicts the requirement that users be able to self-consent to verified, low-risk apps. This forces every request through the admin consent workflow, adding unnecessary administrative overhead.
回答は正解です
Set the user consent setting to "Allow user consent for apps from verified publishers, for selected permissions," and add the approved low-risk permissions to a permission classification labeled "Low."
説明
This answer is correct. The "Allow user consent for apps from verified publishers, for selected permissions" setting lets users consent only when both conditions (verified publisher and specific permissions) are met, and permission classifications let you define exactly which permissions count as "Low" risk. Requests that don't meet these criteria are automatically blocked from user consent and can be routed through the admin consent workflow.
Set the user consent setting to "Allow user consent for apps," and configure the admin consent workflow to notify administrators of every new consent request.
説明
This answer is incorrect. "Allow user consent for apps" permits users to consent to any app regardless of publisher verification or permission risk level, which does not meet the requirement to restrict self-consent to verified publishers and low-risk permissions only. Notifying admins of every request also defeats the goal of avoiding unnecessary admin intervention for approved scenarios.
全体的な説明
Microsoft Entra ID allows granular control of user consent through the "Allow user consent for apps from verified publishers, for selected permissions" setting combined with permission classifications (Low, Medium, High). This combination lets organizations empower users to self-service consent for trusted, low-risk scenarios while ensuring all other requests are escalated through the admin consent workflow, satisfying both security and usability requirements.
